# Frontend Rules

UI code lives in `apps/web` (dashboard, auth, marketing) and `packages/widget` (embeddable chat widget). Follow existing patterns in the codebase.

## shadcn/ui

- Use shadcn/ui components from `apps/web/components/ui/` as the base design system.
- Add new shadcn components via the CLI (`pnpm dlx shadcn@latest add <component>`) rather than copying from external sources.
- Extend shadcn primitives with project-specific variants; do not fork component internals unnecessarily.
- Use Tailwind CSS utility classes and the project's design tokens (`globals.css`, CSS variables).
- Match existing component APIs and naming when creating composite components.

## Accessibility

- Use semantic HTML elements (`button`, `nav`, `main`, `dialog`, `label`) instead of generic `div`/`span` with click handlers.
- Every interactive element must be keyboard accessible (Tab, Enter, Escape, arrow keys where appropriate).
- Provide visible focus indicators; do not remove focus outlines without an accessible replacement.
- Associate form inputs with labels; use `aria-*` attributes when shadcn/Radix primitives require them.
- Ensure color contrast meets WCAG AA for text and interactive elements.
- Test with screen reader semantics in mind: headings hierarchy, live regions for dynamic updates.

## Responsive

- Design mobile-first; scale up with Tailwind responsive prefixes (`sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`).
- Dashboard layouts must work on tablet and desktop; critical flows must work on mobile.
- Avoid fixed pixel widths that break on small screens; prefer flex, grid, and max-width containers.
- Test chat widget and inbox views at common breakpoints (320px, 768px, 1280px).

## Loading States

- Show loading indicators during async operations (data fetching, form submission, file upload).
- Use skeleton loaders for page-level content; spinners or button loading states for actions.
- Disable submit buttons while a mutation is in flight to prevent double submission.
- Prefer React Suspense boundaries and Next.js loading.tsx where appropriate in the App Router.

## Empty States

- Every list, inbox, or collection view must handle the zero-items case with a helpful empty state.
- Empty states should explain what the area is for and guide the user to the next action (e.g., "No conversations yet").
- Do not show a blank screen or an infinite loader when data is simply empty.

## Error States

- Display user-friendly error messages when data fetching or mutations fail.
- Provide retry actions where recovery is possible.
- Do not expose raw error messages, stack traces, or internal codes to users.
- Use error boundaries for unexpected render failures in client components.

## Avoid Duplicated Components

- Before creating a new component, search `apps/web/components/` for an existing one that can be reused or extended.
- Extract shared UI into reusable components when the same pattern appears in two or more places.
- Shared presentation components used by both web and widget belong in a shared package if cross-app reuse is needed.
- Do not copy-paste component code; refactor into a shared abstraction instead.

## Client vs Server Components

- Default to Server Components in the App Router; add `"use client"` only when client interactivity is required.
- Keep client components small; push data fetching and business logic to the server.
- Never import server-only modules (service role, secrets) into client components.

## Forms and Validation

- Use Zod schemas from `packages/shared` for client-side form validation.
- Display field-level validation errors inline next to the relevant input.
- Optimistic UI is acceptable for chat messages but must reconcile with server responses and handle failures gracefully.
